#import "../../../proofs.typ": speech, footnote

== 16. Of Good Works

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 1]: Good works are only such as God hath commanded in His Holy Word,#footnote[Micah 6:8; Hebrews 13:21] and not such as without the warrant thereof are devised by men out of blind zeal, or upon any pretense of good intentions.#footnote[Matthew 15:9; Isaiah 29:13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 2]: These good works, done in obedience to God’s commandments, are the fruits and evidences of a true and lively faith;#footnote[James 2:18, 22] and by them believers manifest their thankfulness,#footnote[Psalm 116:12–13] strengthen their assurance,#footnote[1 John 2:3, 5; 2 Peter 1:5–11] edify their brethren, adorn the profession of the gospel,#footnote[Matthew 5:16] stop the mouths of the adversaries, and glorify God,#footnote[1 Timothy 6:1; 1 Peter 2:15; Philippians 1:11] whose workmanship they are, created in Christ Jesus thereunto,#footnote[Ephesians 2:10] that having their fruit unto holiness they may have the end eternal life.#footnote[Romans 6:22]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 3]: Their ability to do good works is not all of themselves, but wholly from the Spirit of Christ;#footnote[John 15:4–5] and that they may be enabled thereunto, besides the graces they have already received, there is necessary an actual influence of the same Holy Spirit, to work in them and to will and to do of His good pleasure;#footnote[2 Corinthians 3:5; Philippians 2:13] yet they are not hereupon to grow negligent, as if they were not bound to perform any duty, unless upon a special motion of the Spirit, but they ought to be diligent in stirring up the grace of God that is in them.#footnote[Philippians 2:12; Hebrews 6:11–12; Isaiah 64:7]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 4]: They who in their obedience attain to the greatest height which is possible in this life, are so far from being able to supererogate, and to do more than God requires, as that they fall short of much which in duty they are bound to do.#footnote[Job 9:2–3; Galatians 5:17; Luke 17:10]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 5]: We cannot by our best works merit pardon of sin or eternal life at the hand of God, by reason of the great disproportion that is between them and the glory to come, and the infinite distance that is between us and God, whom by them we can neither profit nor satisfy for the debt of our former sins;#footnote[Romans 3:20; Ephesians 2:8–9; Romans 4:6] but when we have done all we can, we have done but our duty, and are unprofitable servants; and because as they are good they proceed from His Spirit,#footnote[Galatians 5:22–23] and as they are wrought by us they are defiled and mixed with so much weakness and imperfection, that they cannot endure the severity of God’s judgement.#footnote[Isaiah 64:6; Psalm 43:2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 6]: Yet notwithstanding the persons of believers being accepted through Christ, their good works also are accepted in Him;#footnote[Ephesians 1:5; 1 Peter 1:5] not as though they were in this life wholly unblamable and unreprovable in God’s sight, but that He, looking upon them in His Son, is pleased to accept and reward that which is sincere, although accompanied with many weaknesses and imperfection.#footnote[Matthew 25:21, 23; Hebrews 6:10]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 7]: Works done by unregenerate men, although for the matter of them they may things which God commands, and of good use both to themselves and to others;#footnote[2 Kings 10:30; 1 Kings 21:27, 29] yet because they proceed not from a heart purified by faith,#footnote[Genesis 4:5; Hebrews 11:4, 6] nor are done in a right manner according to the Word,#footnote[1 Corinthians 13:1] nor to a right end, the glory of God,#footnote[Matthew 6:2, 5] they are therefore sinful, and cannot please God, nor make a man meet to receive the grace from God,#footnote[Amos 5:21–22; Romans 9:16; Titus 3:5] and yet their neglect for them is more sinful and displeasing to God.#footnote[Job 21:14–15; Matthew 25:41–43]]
